venu-pacific (26.08.15) trixie; urgency=medium

  * venu-pacific-assistant: remove the translate-to-Bislama/Fijian buttons
    added in 26.08.14. Pilot testing showed the model cannot translate into
    either language: Fijian came out with Samoan morphology and Bislama
    parroted the prompt's example phrases instead of translating. Verified
    against the real model with few-shot prompts built from the reviewed
    translation catalogs -- prompting does not create a capability the
    model lacks. A dedicated button endorses output the way free-form chat
    does not, and a confident wrong-language rendering of safety text is
    worse than no button. Free-form translation in chat remains, covered by
    the existing accuracy disclaimer. The calculate tool from 26.08.14 is
    unaffected and stays.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:31:22 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * venu-pacific-assistant: new calculate tool. Pilot testing caught the
    model answering a question about 8 textbooks with the numbers for 9 --
    a language model predicts digits rather than computing them. Arithmetic
    now goes through an exact evaluator (ast-based with a strict whitelist,
    never eval) that handles the notation the model actually writes:
    unicode operators, thousands separators, caret powers, percentages.
    The prompt directs the model to use it for anything beyond a trivial
    single step.
  * One-tap translation under every assistant reply: Bislama and Fijian
    buttons that send the reply back through the normal model path as a
    translation request. The reply text is embedded in the prompt so the
    buttons are also correct on older messages in a reloaded conversation.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:10:27 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The offline-install fix in 26.08.11 never reached any image. It left
    the bootloader .debs in /var/cache/apt/archives, and live-build's
    chroot_archives remove step deletes that entire directory after the
    chroot stage -- verified by building 26.08.12 and finding zero cached
    debs in the squashfs. The hook had even reported success, because
    live-build restores its whole multi-gigabyte build cache into the same
    directory, so "are there debs here" was true for the wrong reason.
  * The debs now live in /opt/venu-pacific/offline-debs, which nothing
    cleans, and the hook patches Calamares' bootloader helper to copy them
    into the target's apt cache just before its apt-get install runs. The
    hook fails the build unless both grub-efi-amd64 and grub-pc are present
    by name and the patched helper passes a syntax check. Online installs
    behave exactly as before; the preload is additive.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 03:05:59 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Learn (Kolibri) no longer reports a false failure. The launcher asked
    "kolibri status" whether the server was running, but status exits
    non-zero both when Kolibri is stopped AND while it is still starting
    up, and startup takes 20-40s on this hardware. A second click during
    that window -- what an impatient user does when the first click seems
    to do nothing -- was read as "not running", tried to start a second
    server, and Kolibri correctly refused. The user then saw "could not
    start, try restarting the computer" about a server that was working
    and about to open. Found from a real ~/.kolibri/logs/kolibri.txt on
    the pilot machine.
  * It now asks whether anything is serving HTTP on the port, which is the
    only thing it actually needs to know and is true exactly when opening
    the browser will work. A refused start is no longer fatal: already
    starting is a success from the user's point of view.
  * Kolibri's output goes to ~/.cache/venu-pacific-learn.log instead of
    /dev/null, and the timeout dialog names that file. Discarding it is why
    this took a log file on the pilot machine to diagnose at all.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 02:12:53 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Installing to disk now works without an internet connection. Calamares'
    Debian helper runs "apt-get install grub-efi" (or grub-pc) near the end
    of every install, and neither was in the image, so apt had to fetch it.
    Offline that failed and cascaded: no bootloader package meant no
    /etc/default/grub and no update-grub, so the install aborted around 80%
    with "failure resolving 'deb.debian.org'" and two follow-on errors that
    tell the user nothing they can act on. Found on real hardware.
  * Preinstalling one is not an option -- grub-efi Conflicts grub-pc and the
    target machines are a mix of UEFI and BIOS. Both are now left in apt's
    local cache instead, which unpackfs copies onto the target with the rest
    of the filesystem, so apt installs from a local file. Verified offline
    for both the UEFI and BIOS paths. Costs about 15MB.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 01:25:48 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Live session no longer identifies itself as Debian. live-config's own
    defaults are LIVE_HOSTNAME="debian", LIVE_USERNAME="user" and
    LIVE_USER_FULLNAME="Debian Live user", and nothing had ever overridden
    them: the XFCE panel named the user "Debian Live user" in the top-right
    corner, and every terminal prompt read user@debian. Now venu, on
    venu-pacific, shown as "Venu Pacific Live User".
  * Ships through includes.chroot rather than a package because live-config
    only runs on the live session; an installed machine gets its name and
    hostname from Calamares instead.

